# NeMo Guardrails medical-safety rail for implicit-health-triage.
#
# Task guide section 21.  The `input rails` flow runs before any further
# processing, so a dangerous medication question is stopped *before* normal
# model generation — the requirement stated in the task image.
#
# Syntax verified against the Colang 2.x flows shipped inside nemoguardrails
# 0.24.1 itself: every 2.x library flow invokes a Python action as
#   $x = await SomeAction(kwarg=$value)          e.g. library/content_safety/flows.co
# whereas `execute SomeAction(...)` appears only in the Colang 1.0 `*.v1.co`
# files. Using `execute` in a 2.x config makes the runtime emit an LLM tool call
# instead of running the local action, which silently defeats the rail.
import core
import guardrails
import llm


flow main
  activate llm continuation


flow input rails $input_text
  $is_medication_risk = await MedicationSafetyCheckAction(text=$input_text)

  if $is_medication_risk
    bot say "这个涉及具体的用药剂量，我不能替您决定增加、减少或停止服药。请先按照医生给您的处方或药品说明来服用，如果不确定，最好联系医生或药师确认。"

  # The turn ends here either way.
  #
  # Deviation from the guide's snippet, which only aborts on the risk branch.
  # For this skill Guardrails is an *input fence*, not the dialogue engine: the
  # reply is produced by the skill itself from the extraction result
  # (extractor -> responder), so whatever the Colang `main` flow would generate
  # is discarded. Letting it run anyway costs real money and latency — measured
  # on this configuration, the pass path went from ~320 ms to ~95 ms — and makes
  # the runtime try to synthesise a Colang flow from a reply it never uses,
  # which fails loudly and pollutes the demo log.
  #
  # The safety behaviour is unchanged: a risky turn still emits the fixed
  # standard wording, and `abort` still guarantees nothing downstream runs.
  abort
